{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000754","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for lupus erythematosus (including multiple systemic lupus erythematosus models, plus two labeled broadly as lupus erythematosus). However, most candidates either (a) report only full-model AUROC without PRS-only metrics, making cross-model comparison weak, or (b) have phenotype restrictions/packaging that reduce endpoint fidelity for generic lupus erythematosus (e.g., early-onset-only, renal/nephritis subphenotypes, or a model adjusted for a phenotype risk score).

PGS000754 is preferred because it has strong endpoint fidelity for generic SLE (phenotyping_reported: systemic lupus erythematosus), uses a genome-wide shrinkage-style method (lassosum) with a very large variant set (293,684), which is a positive structural signal relative to the many GWAS-hit scores with <100 variants. Its reported AUROC (0.65) is not exceptional, but given that this is still a full-model metric and covariates are unknown across many candidates, the structural advantages and direct disease endpoint are the most consistent support.

Main limitation: no candidate provides clearly comparable PRS-only performance (PGS-only AUROC/R2) in the provided metadata, and several models show higher full-model AUROC (e.g., PGS003960) but with explicit non-genetic packaging/covariates (phenotype risk score) that domain guidance flags as leakage, so the overall best choice remains uncertain."}
